  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 310 PM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThe axis of an upper-level trough positioned over the Great Lakes\nregion will shift eastward and deepen slightly through Friday \nbefore pulling off toward the northeast into the weekend. On \nFriday, a surface front is likely to stall over the north to \ncentral tier of our CWA, acting as a focal point for multiple \nrounds of scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms. \nPrecipitable water values will be in excess of 2 inches over the \narea, supporting heavy rainfall totals and localized flash \nflooding considerations through the first half of the weekend. In \nline with this, the Weather Prediction Center has our area in a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall, so we will need to monitor \nrainfall rates with some of these storms. Additionally, the Storm \nPrediction Center does bring a marginal risk of severe weather \ninto the north-northeast portions of our CWA on Friday, with \npossible damaging wind gusts being the primary threat. With ample \ncloud cover and a wetter overall pattern, daytime high \ntemperatures will be held down slightly, ranging from the mid-80s \nto low-90s across the area through the end of the weekend. \n\nRain and thunderstorm chances begin to decrease as we move into \nthe second half of the weekend, as a broad upper-level ridge \nbegins to build and shift over the area by early next week. In \nresponse, high temperatures will climb back into the mid-90s, with\ndewpoints in the 70s with possible heat advisory considerations \nby mid next week. So if you enjoy summertime weather, we remain \nlocked in this pattern with diurnally driven thunderstorms for the\nextended forecast.",
